    85	## 1. Cohomology of the normal hypersurface
    86	
    87	Put `W=P2 times P1`.  Because `X` is a Cartier divisor of class `(2,3)`,
    88	
    89	```text
    90	0 -> O_W(-2,-3) -> O_W -> O_X -> 0.                 (1.1)
    91	```
    92	
    93	Every cohomology group of `O_P2(-2)` vanishes: the top group is dual to
    94	`H^0(O_P2(-1))`, and the intermediate group vanishes.  Kunneth therefore
    95	gives
    96	
    97	```text
    98	H^i(W,O_W(-2,-3))=0       for every i.               (1.2)
    99	```
   100	
   101	Since `H^1(W,O_W)=H^2(W,O_W)=0`, the long exact sequence gives the exact
   102	surface statements
   103	
   104	```text
   105	H^1(X,O_X)=0,             H^2(X,O_X)=0.              (1.3)
   106	```
   107	
   108	Normality implies `r_*O_Xtilde=O_X`, and the singular set is finite.  Hence
   109	`R^1r_*O_Xtilde` is a finite-support sheaf.  The Leray five-term sequence,
   110	using (1.3), is
   111	
   112	```text
   113	H^1(Xtilde,O_Xtilde)  isomorphic to
   114	H^0(X,R^1r_*O_Xtilde),
   115	H^2(Xtilde,O_Xtilde)=0.                              (1.4)
   116	```
   117	
   118	Equation (1.4) is the required bridge: vanishing of `q(Xtilde)` is exactly
   119	what turns the hypersurface cohomology into rational singularities.  The
   120	vanishing (1.3) alone would not suffice.
   121	
   122	## 2. Why the resolution is rational and the singularities are rational
   123	
   124	There are two independent routes to `q(Xtilde)=0` in the declared scope.
   125	
   126	First, the dominant first-leg morphism `A2->U subset X_sm` induces a dominant
   127	rational map to `Xtilde`.  Compactifying the source and resolving
   128	indeterminacy makes `Xtilde` unirational.  In characteristic zero a smooth
   129	projective unirational surface has `p_g=q=0` (indeed it is rational by the
   130	Castelnuovo criterion).  Thus the first-leg bridge already supplies the left
   131	side of (1.4) as zero.
   132	
   133	Second, the incidence itself gives a direct rationality check.  Projection
   134	
   135	```text
   136	q:X->P1                                                   (2.1)
   137	```
   138	
   139	has generic fibre a plane conic.  That conic is smooth.  To see the
   140	load-bearing use of normality, represent it by a symmetric matrix `M(t)`.
   141	If `det M` vanished identically and `M` had generic rank two, a kernel vector
   142	`v` would satisfy all fibre derivatives.  Jacobi's formula gives
   143	
   144	```text
   145	0=(det M)'=trace(adj(M)M')=lambda*v^t*M'*v,
   146	```
   147	
   148	so the base derivative vanishes at the same generic point.  Its closure is a
   149	curve in `Sing(X)`, contrary to normality.  Generic rank at most one has a
   150	positive-dimensional fibre singular locus.  More explicitly, after a finite
   151	extension of the function field a generic rank-one form is `lambda*l^2`;
   152	both its fibre derivatives and its base derivative vanish along `l=0`.
   153	That again produces a singular curve dominating the base, incompatible with
   154	regularity in codimension one.  Generic rank zero is impossible for the
   155	irreducible hypersurface.  Thus the generic conic is smooth.  Tsen's theorem
   156	gives it a `C(P1)`-point, so it is `P1` over the function field and `Xtilde`
   157	is rational.
   158	
   159	Therefore `H^1(Xtilde,O_Xtilde)=0`.  Equation (1.4) gives
   160	
   161	```text
   162	H^0(R^1r_*O_Xtilde)=0.
   163	```
   164	
   165	A nonzero coherent sheaf of finite support has a nonzero global section, so
   166	
   167	```text
   168	R^1r_*O_Xtilde=0.                                    (2.2)
   169	```
   170	
   171	This is precisely rationality of every normal surface singularity of `X`.
   172	
   173	## 3. Gorenstein rational implies Du Val and crepant
   174	
   175	The hypersurface `X` is Gorenstein.  Dualizing adjunction, valid for the
   176	normal Cartier hypersurface, gives
   177	
   178	```text
   179	omega_X=O_X(-A+B),       K_X=-A+B.                   (3.1)
   180	```
   181	
   182	Over `C`, a normal rational Gorenstein surface singularity is a rational
   183	double point, equivalently a Du Val singularity.  Hence the exceptional
   184	curves of the minimal resolution form a disjoint union of ADE Dynkin trees,
   185	every irreducible exceptional curve has square `-2`, and
   186	
   187	```text
   188	K_Xtilde=r^*K_X=-A+B.                                (3.2)
   189	```
   190	
   191	Thus the resolution is crepant.  Ambient intersection gives
   192	
   193	```text
   194	A^2=3,       A.B=2,       B^2=0,
   195	K_Xtilde^2=(-A+B)^2=-1.                              (3.3)
   196	```
   197	
   198	Rationality gives `chi(O_Xtilde)=1`.  Noether's formula and Hodge theory now
   199	give
   200	
   201	```text
   202	c_2(Xtilde)=e_top(Xtilde)=12*chi-K^2=13,
   203	b_2(Xtilde)=11,
   204	rho(Xtilde)=11.                                      (3.4)
   205	```
   206	
   207	Here `rho=11` is the Picard rank of the smooth resolution, not the Weil
   208	class-group rank of the singular surface.
   209	
   210	## 4. The ruled nine-blowup marking
   211	
   212	The morphism `q o r:Xtilde->P1` has smooth rational generic fibre and a
   213	section by Tsen and properness.  Its fibres are connected by Stein
   214	factorization, and the section excludes multiple fibres.  Contract vertical
   215	`-1` curves.  The relative minimal-model theorem for a genus-zero fibration
   216	with a section identifies the endpoint with a `P1`-bundle, hence with a
   217	Hirzebruch surface `F_e`: a reducible genus-zero fibre on a smooth surface
   218	contains a vertical `-1` component, while the section has already excluded
   219	multiple fibres, so the relatively minimal endpoint has only smooth `P1`
   220	fibres.  Thus this is a geometrically constructed marking, not a postulated
   221	abstract Picard-lattice orbit.  The reverse map expresses `Xtilde` as
   222	blowups at points of fibres, possibly infinitely near.
   223	
   224	Since `K_(F_e)^2=8` and every blowup lowers `K^2` by one, (3.3) shows that
   225	there are exactly nine blowups.  Use total-transform classes
   226	
   227	```text
   228	S^2=-e,       S.F=1,       F^2=0,
   229	E_i^2=-1,     S.E_i=F.E_i=E_i.E_j=0  (i!=j),         (4.1)
   230	```
   231	
   232	where `F=B` and `i=1,...,9`.  The canonical class is
   233	
   234	```text
   235	K_Xtilde=-2S-(e+2)F+sum_(i=1)^9 E_i.                (4.2)
   236	```
   237	
   238	Write
   239	
   240	```text
   241	A=2S+bF+sum m_iE_i,
   242	```
   243	
   244	where the coefficient of `S` is forced by `A.F=2`.  Comparing (4.2) with
   245	the crepant equality `K_Xtilde=-A+F` determines every coefficient, without
   246	an effectivity assumption:
   247	
   248	```text
   249	A=2S+(e+3)F-sum_(i=1)^9 E_i.                        (4.3)
   250	```
   251	
   252	Equations (4.1)--(4.3) recover `A^2=3` and `A.F=2`.  They remain valid when
   253	some blowup centres are infinitely near; the `E_i` are total-transform
   254	lattice classes, not a claim that all nine are simultaneously irreducible
   255	exceptional curves.  Moreover these eleven classes are an integral basis of
   256	`NS(Xtilde)`: each vertical contraction is an actual blowdown and each
   257	reverse step adjoins its total-transform exceptional class.  There is no
   258	unmentioned finite-index overlattice in this marking.
   259	
   260	The marking has the further inexpensive bound
   261	
   262	```text
   263	0<=e<=3.                                             (4.4)
   264	```
   265	
   266	Indeed the total transform of the negative section of `F_e` is effective,
   267	`A` is nef, and (4.1)--(4.3) give `A.S=3-e`.
   268	
   269	## 5. The orthogonal complement is `D9`, not an unproved guess
   270	
   271	Let
   272	
   273	```text
   274	L=(ZA+ZF)^perp subset NS(Xtilde).                    (5.1)
   275	```
   276	
   277	Write a general class as
   278	
   279	```text
   280	x=aS+bF+sum c_iE_i.
   281	```
   282	
   283	The equation `x.F=0` gives `a=0`, and (4.3) then gives
   284	
   285	```text
   286	x.A=2b+sum c_i=0.                                    (5.2)
   287	```
   288	
   289	Thus `b` is integral exactly when `sum c_i` is even, and
   290	
   291	```text
   292	x^2=-sum c_i^2.                                      (5.3)
   293	```
   294	
   295	The map `x |-> (c_1,...,c_9)` is therefore an explicit integral isometry
   296	
   297	```text
   298	L isomorphic to D9(-1)
   299	 = {(c_i) in Z^9 : sum c_i even},
   300	   pairing -sum c_i c_i'.                            (5.4)
   301	```
   302	
   303	In particular `L` is even, negative definite, rank nine, and determinant
   304	four.  The pair `ZA+ZF` is primitive in `NS(Xtilde)`: in the basis (4.1),
   305	the two-by-two minor using the `F` and any `E_i` rows has determinant one.
   306	Thus (5.4) is the actual orthogonal complement of the geometric primitive
   307	pair, rather than merely a finite-index candidate.
   308	No transitivity theorem for ruled markings is being used: one actual
   309	relative blowdown supplies an integral basis in which the intrinsic lattice
   310	`L` is computed, and the calculation is independent of `e` and of the
   311	chosen blowdown order.
   312	
   313	The abstract data “even, negative definite, rank nine, determinant four” do
   314	**not** alone prove (5.4).  For example
   315	
   316	```text
   317	E8(-1) direct-sum <-4>                               (5.5)
   318	```
   319	
   320	has the same four properties.
   321	
   322	The discriminant form also does not distinguish these two lattices.  For
   323	`D9(-1)`, the class of
   324	
   325	```text
   326	g=(1/2,...,1/2)
   327	```
   328	
   329	generates the order-four discriminant group and has
   330	`q(g)=-9/4=-1/4 mod 2Z`.  The generator of the discriminant group of the
   331	`<-4>` summand in (5.5) has square `-1/4`.  Hence both discriminant forms are
   332	the cyclic order-four form with value `-1/4`; primitivity and discriminant
   333	data alone are still inconclusive.
   334	
   335	The explicit marking already decides between them.  As a further invariant
   336	check, the roots of (5.4) are exactly
   337	
   338	```text
   339	plus-or-minus e_i plus-or-minus e_j,       i!=j,
   340	```
   341	
   342	so there are `4*binomial(9,2)=144` and they span rank nine.  In (5.5), every
   343	square-`-2` vector lies in the `E8(-1)` summand; there are 240 such roots and
   344	they span only rank eight inside the rank-nine lattice.  Hence (5.4) and
   345	(5.5) are not isometric.  The `E8 direct-sum <-4>` alternative is explicitly
   346	refuted here, not silently discarded.
   347	
   348	## 6. What the ADE exceptional lattice actually inherits
   349	
   350	Let `Lambda_exc` be the lattice generated by the irreducible curves
   351	exceptional for `r`.  Pullback divisors have zero intersection with every
   352	such curve, so
   353	
   354	```text
   355	Lambda_exc subset L=D9(-1).                          (6.1)
   356	```
   357	
   358	By Section 3, `Lambda_exc` is a direct sum of negative ADE root lattices.
   359	Therefore the exact conclusions are only:
   360	
   361	```text
   362	rank Lambda_exc <= 9,
   363	Lambda_exc is an integral root sublattice of D9(-1),
   364	every exceptional curve is vertical for Xtilde->P1. (6.2)
   365	```
   366	
   367	The inclusion need not be primitive and need not have full rank.  No
   368	determinant quotient may be taken without an index calculation.  In
   369	particular `Lambda_exc=D9(-1)` is **not** promoted.
   370	
   371	One additional exclusion is immediate and self-contained: an `E8(-1)` root
   372	lattice cannot embed isometrically in `D9(-1)`, because its 240 distinct
   373	roots would have to map to 240 distinct roots while `D9` has only 144.
   374	Thus an `E8` singularity cannot occur in this normal incidence/first-leg
   375	scope (indeed the first leg is not needed for this exclusion).  No analogous
   376	exclusion of `E6` or `E7`, and no classification of all
   377	root sublattices of `D9`, is asserted here.
   378	
   379	## 7. Crepant ramification data and its exact limit
   380	
   381	The map `pi:X->P2` is generically finite of degree `A^2=3`; in characteristic
   382	zero it is generically separable.  On the smooth locus of `X`, the
   383	determinant of `d pi` is therefore a nonzero section of
   384	
   385	```text
   386	omega_X tensor pi^*omega_P2^(-1)=O_X(2A+B).          (7.1)
   387	```
   388	
   389	Normality extends this section across the finite singular set.  Its divisor
   390	`R_X` is a nonzero effective Cartier divisor in the ample class `2A+B`.
   391	An effective ample Cartier divisor on a normal projective surface has
   392	connected support.  Crepancy identifies the ramification divisor of
   393	`pi o r` with the total Cartier pullback:
   394	
   395	```text
   396	R_Xtilde=r^*R_X ~ 2A+B.                              (7.2)
   397	```
   398	
   399	In the ruled marking this class is exactly
   400	
   401	```text
   402	R_Xtilde ~ 4S+(2e+7)F-2sum_(i=1)^9 E_i.
   403	```
   404	
   405	Consequently
   406	
   407	```text
   408	R_Xtilde^2=20,       A.R_Xtilde=8,       B.R_Xtilde=4,
   409	R_Xtilde.C=0 for every r-exceptional curve C.         (7.3)
   410	```
   411	
   412	Its total support is connected.  On `Xtilde`, however, `2A+B` is only nef
   413	and big: it has zero intersection with all ADE exceptional curves.  It is
   414	not ample when `X` is singular.  Crepancy also does not mean that exceptional
   415	curves are absent from the total different.  In fact every `r`-exceptional
   416	curve occurs in `R_Xtilde`: the map `pi o r` contracts that curve to a point,
   417	so its differential has rank at most one at the generic point of the curve
   418	and its determinant vanishes there.  Consequently `R_X` passes through every
   419	singular point of `X`.
   420	
   421	There is a useful exact local equation, but not yet a classification.  Over
   422	one singular point, write
   423	
   424	```text
   425	R_Xtilde=R_str+sum_i m_i E_i,       m_i>=1,          (7.4)
   426	```
   427	
   428	where the `E_i` form its ADE tree and `R_str` has no exceptional component.
   429	Set `n_i=R_str.E_i>=0`.  Intersecting (7.4) with `E_i` and using (7.3) gives
   430	
   431	```text
   432	n_i=2m_i-sum_(j adjacent to i) m_j.                 (7.5)
   433	```
   434	
   435	Thus, in positive Cartan notation, `n=C_ADE*m`.  The attachment vector `n`
   436	cannot vanish: the ADE Cartan matrix is nonsingular, whereas every `m_i` is
   437	positive.  Hence the nonexceptional ramification meets every exceptional
   438	tree at least once, counted with intersection multiplicity.  Equations
   439	(7.4)--(7.5) do not determine `m`, the number of physical attachment points,
   440	or which nonexceptional components carry those attachments.
   441	
   442	This is exactly where the smooth connectedness/attachment proof stops.  The
   443	connected total support in (7.2) and the nonzero attachment vector (7.5) are
   444	useful, but without the local pullback multiplicities and physical attachment
   445	points through each ADE tree they do not supply two independent boundary
   446	paths.
   447	
   448	## 8. Hostile checks and false shortcuts
   449	
   450	The reduction survives the following attacks.
   451	
   452	* **`H^1(O_X)=H^2(O_X)=0` alone proves rational singularities.**  False.
   453	  The missing term is `H^1(O_Xtilde)`.  Section 2 kills it by the first leg
   454	  and independently by the conic/Tsen rationality argument before using
   455	  Leray.
   456	* **Normality is cosmetic.**  False.  It makes the singular set finite,
   457	  supplies `r_*O=O`, and prevents a generically singular conic whose singular
   458	  locus would dominate the base.  None of Sections 1--3 is transferred to a
   459	  nonnormal closure.
   460	* **Rational automatically means Du Val.**  False without Gorenstein.  The
   461	  hypersurface Gorenstein property is the additional input.
   462	* **Crepant means the exceptional curves do not occur in the different.**
   463	  False.  It identifies the total pullback class and rational section; local
   464	  Cartier pullback multiplicities remain.
   465	* **Rank nine and determinant four imply `D9`.**  False, with (5.5) as the
   466	  explicit control.  The ruled nine-blowup marking proves `D9`; the root
   467	  counts independently distinguish the candidates.
   468	* **The exceptional lattice is the orthogonal complement.**  False.  It is
   469	  only a possibly nonprimitive ADE root sublattice.
   470	* **The old smooth conic-bundle marking can be copied verbatim.**  The total
   471	  transform lattice marking survives, but its effective `E_i` curves and
   472	  fibre components may be altered by infinitely near centres and ADE
   473	  resolutions.  Effectivity must be rebuilt.
   474	* **Connected ramification already gives a cycle.**  Not without two typed
   475	  physical attachments after resolving the ADE trees.
   476	
   477	No CAS, finite-field sampling, or AWS computation is used.
   478	
   479	## 9. Exact remaining gap and scope firewall
   480	
   481	The next finite object is an **ADE-decorated boundary/different lattice**, not
   482	a search over arbitrary Picard groups.  It must determine, for every allowed
   483	root sublattice `Lambda_exc subset D9(-1)`:
   484	
   485	1. the reduced strict transform and Cartier multiplicities of infinity
   486	   `H~A`, including nonreduced leading forms and projective coefficient
   487	   basepoints;
   488	2. the total pullback of `R_X~2A+B` through each ADE tree, with local
   489	   intersection and different multiplicities;
   490	3. which exceptional vertices belong to the completed first-leg boundary;
   491	4. the physical attachment points between the connected different support
   492	   and the resolved infinity tree; and
   493	5. effectivity and irreducibility of all nonexceptional carrier classes in
   494	   the ruled nine-blowup marking.
   495	
   496	Only after those data are typed can the rational-forest cycle test or the
   497	smooth `F5` lattice enumeration be reused.  The present packet supplies no
   498	normal-singular exclusion and no occurrence theorem.
   499	
   500	The hypotheses are: characteristic zero; an irreducible normal Cartier
   501	hypersurface of class `2A+3B`; the promoted proper cubic block/first-leg
   502	scope when the first-leg route is invoked; and the minimal resolution.
   503	Nonnormal incidence closures are wholly outside: their normalization,
   504	conductor curve, cohomology sequence, and generic-conic singular locus need
   505	separate treatment.  Also outside are basis minimization, a proof that an
   506	arbitrary cubic block has a quadratic presentation, higher coefficient
   507	degree, another block degree, block primitivity, a polynomial map, a
   508	counterexample, and JC2.
