    52	>    or support of `n`.  A tangent contact of large numerical multiplicity is
    53	>    one joining path.  A branch through an ADE node can contribute at both
    54	>    adjacent graph vertices while still being one physical attachment.
    55	> 4. No remaining connected type is eliminated by these numerical and local
    56	>    forest tests.  Every `A_1,...,A_8` and `D_4,...,D_9` has an explicit
    57	>    Cartier-lattice one-germ pattern with `sum n_i<=2`.  “Survives” here means only
    58	>    that these necessary tests do not kill it; neither an embedding by
    59	>    effective exceptional curves nor an incidence equation is constructed.
    60	
    61	The actual finite successor is therefore not another unrestricted lattice
    62	search.  It is the effectivity and physical-attachment test on the explicit
    63	finite congruence sets, together with a separate treatment of nonfinite or
    64	common-component strata where the intersection cap is unavailable.
    65	
    66	## 1. Local Cartier equations on an ADE tree
    67	
    68	Fix a singular point `p` and number the irreducible exceptional curves
    69	`E_1,...,E_r`.  Let `C` be the positive ADE Cartan matrix, so
    70	
    71	```text
    72	E_i.E_i=-2,        E_i.E_j=1 for adjacent vertices,
    73	(E_i.E_j)=-C.                                      (1.1)
    74	```
    75	
    76	Write the total different over `p` as
    77	
    78	```text
    79	R=R_str+sum_i m_i E_i,       m_i>=1,                (1.2)
    80	```
    81	
    82	and define
    83	
    84	```text
    85	n_i=R_str.E_i>=0.                                   (1.3)
    86	```
    87	
    88	Every `m_i` is positive because `pi o r` contracts `E_i`, so its Jacobian
    89	determinant vanishes at the generic point of `E_i`.  Since `R.E_i=0`,
    90	
    91	```text
    92	n=Cm.                                               (1.4)
    93	```
    94	
    95	Conversely, at the level of necessary lattice data, (1.4) gives a bijection
    96	
    97	```text
    98	{m in Z_(>0)^r : Cm>=0}
    99	  <--> {n in Z_(>=0)^r minus {0} : n in C Z^r},
   100	m=C^(-1)n.                                          (1.5)
   101	```
   102	
   103	Indeed every entry of the inverse of a connected finite ADE Cartan matrix is
   104	strictly positive.  Thus a nonzero nonnegative integral `n` has positive
   105	rational inverse, and it gives an integral `m` exactly when `n in C Z^r`.
   106	This is a classification of the numerical solutions, not a converse
   107	existence theorem for a Cartier prime or an incidence singularity.
   108	In particular, (1.4) by itself kills no ADE type: at any vertex `j`, take
   109	`n=k e_j` with `k` divisible by `det C` (or merely by the order of the
   110	corresponding fundamental-weight class).  Then `m=C^(-1)n` is positive and
   111	integral, and scaling `k` gives an unbounded ray.  Every finite conclusion
   112	below therefore cites a separate global proper-intersection cap or the
   113	explicit `D9` embedding.
   114	
   115	Infinity has the same local arithmetic.  If `p in H`, write
   116	
   117	```text
   118	r^*H=H_str+sum_i h_iE_i,       h_i>=1,
   119	a_i=H_str.E_i,                 a=Ch>=0.              (1.6)
   120	```
   121	
   122	Thus the boundary and different decorations are two points of the same
   123	dominant root-lattice semigroup, with separately recorded physical branches.
   124	
   125	## 2. Which connected ADE types can occur in `D9`
   126	
   127	The roots of the lattice (0.2) are exactly
   128	
   129	```text
   130	plus-or-minus e_u plus-or-minus e_v,       u!=v.     (2.1)
   131	```
   132	
   133	There is a short coordinate proof that a connected root lattice generated by
   134	such roots is classical.  Map a simple-root basis into (2.1), and form a
   135	signed graph whose coordinate positions are vertices and whose simple roots
   136	are edges.  Connectedness of the Dynkin diagram forces the used coordinate
   137	graph to be connected.  Switch coordinate signs along a spanning tree so
   138	that its edge roots are differences `e_u-e_v`.
   139	
   140	If `s` coordinate positions occur, those spanning-tree roots generate the
   141	full `A_(s-1)` lattice.  The image rank is between `s-1` and `s`.  In rank
   142	`s-1`, every remaining edge is balanced and the generated lattice is exactly
   143	`A_(s-1)`.  In rank `s`, some edge is unbalanced; adjoining its sum root to
   144	the tree differences generates exactly `D_s`.  There is no third case.
   145	
   146	Since only nine coordinates are available, every connected exceptional type
   147	is therefore among
   148	
   149	```text
   150	A_1,...,A_8,        D_4,...,D_9.                    (2.2)
   151	```
   152	
   153	This excludes `A9,E6,E7,E8`.  Conversely the standard difference roots on
   154	`r+1<=9` coordinates embed each `A_r` in (2.2), and the standard signed roots
   155	on `r<=9` coordinates embed each `D_r`.  These are **lattice-eligibility**
   156	statements only.  The actual exceptional curves may fail the effective-cone,
   157	fibre-configuration, or simultaneous-embedding tests.
   158	
   159	For a disconnected exceptional lattice, apply the argument to every
   160	connected Dynkin component.  Orthogonal components can share coordinate
   161	support in special ways, so their ranks alone do not certify a simultaneous
   162	embedding.  One further exact gate is useful: if the total exceptional
   163	lattice has rank nine, then
   164	
   165	```text
   166	det(Lambda_exc)=4*[D9:Lambda_exc]^2.                 (2.3)
   167	```
   168	
   169	Any proposed full-rank direct sum whose determinant is not four times a
   170	square is impossible.  No primitivity is assumed in (2.3).
   171	
   172	## 3. Exact `A_r` multiplicities
   173	
   174	Number the `A_r` chain `1-2-...-r`.  The cokernel of its Cartan matrix is
   175	cyclic of order `r+1`, and (1.5) becomes the explicit congruence
   176	
   177	```text
   178	sum_(i=1)^r i*n_i = 0 mod (r+1).                    (3.1)
   179	```
   180	
   181	This is exact: the displayed weighted sum kills every column of `C`, and it
   182	is surjective because the first coordinate has weight one.  For every
   183	nonzero `n>=0` satisfying (3.1), the unique positive multiplicities are
   184	
   185	```text
   186	m_j = ((r+1-j)*sum_(i<=j) i*n_i
   187	       +j*sum_(i>j) (r+1-i)*n_i)/(r+1).             (3.2)
   188	```
   189	
   190	In particular, a contact supported numerically at a smooth point of vertex
   191	`i`, so `n=k e_i`, is Cartier exactly when
   192	
   193	```text
   194	k is a multiple of o_i=(r+1)/gcd(i,r+1).            (3.3)
   195	```
   196	
   197	Under the cap `sum n_i<=8`, every vertex of `A_1,...,A_7` permits such a
   198	one-vertex contact, while `A8` permits it only at vertices `3,6`.  Under the
   199	sharper cap four, the vertices permitting at least one such contact are
   200	
   201	```text
   202	A1: {1}                 A2: {1,2}
   203	A3: {1,2,3}             A4: empty
   204	A5: {2,3,4}             A6: empty
   205	A7: {2,4,6}             A8: {3,6}.                 (3.4)
   206	```
   207	
   208	An empty entry in (3.4) does **not** kill the singularity: it only kills a
   209	one-germ contact at a smooth point of the minimal ADE divisor.  At the
   210	numerical Cartier/curvette level there is a uniform one-physical-germ
   211	survivor for every `A_r`:
   212	
   213	```text
   214	r=2k-1:  n=2e_k,
   215	          m=(1,2,...,k-1,k,k-1,...,2,1);
   216	
   217	r=2k:    n=e_k+e_(k+1),
   218	          m=(1,2,...,k-1,k,k,k-1,...,2,1).          (3.5)
   219	```
   220	
   221	The odd-rank pattern is one order-two tangency at the smooth point of the
   222	central exceptional curve.  The even-rank pattern is one irreducible branch
   223	through the node between the two central exceptional curves, contributing
   224	one to each adjacent entry of `n`.  Blowing up that node turns the contact
   225	into one leaf path.  Both patterns have `sum n_i=2` and satisfy (3.1).
   226	
   227	More generally, one irreducible physical branch meeting the node `i--(i+1)`
   228	has
   229	
   230	```text
   231	n=u e_i+v e_(i+1),       u,v>=1,                    (3.6)
   232	```
   233	
   234	and is numerically Cartier exactly when
   235	`i*u+(i+1)*v=0 mod (r+1)`.  This makes the distinction between graph support
   236	and physical-point count explicit.
   237	
   238	## 4. Exact `D_r` multiplicities
   239	
   240	Use the numbering
   241	
   242	```text
   243	1--2--...--(r-2),
   244	              |--(r-1)
   245	              |--r,                                 (4.1)
   246	```
   247	
   248	and put `a=n_(r-1)`, `b=n_r`, and
   249	`T=sum_(i=1)^(r-2) i*n_i`.  The condition `n in C Z^r` is exactly
   250	
   251	```text
   252	a=b mod 2,
   253	2T+(r-2)a+r*b=0 mod 4.                              (4.2)
   254	```
   255	
   256	The inverse formulas are
   257	
   258	```text
   259	m_j=sum_(i=1)^(r-2) min(i,j)*n_i+j*(a+b)/2,
   260	                                      1<=j<=r-2,
   261	m_(r-1)=T/2+r*a/4+(r-2)*b/4,
   262	m_r    =T/2+(r-2)*a/4+r*b/4.                        (4.3)
   263	```
   264	
   265	Thus (4.2), nonnegativity, and nonzero `n` classify all positive integral
   266	solutions of (1.4).  For a smooth-vertex contact `n=k e_i`, the least
   267	possible `k` is
   268	
   269	```text
   270	i<=r-2:  1 if i is even, 2 if i is odd;
   271	i=r-1,r: 2 if r is even, 4 if r is odd.             (4.4)
   272	```
   273	
   274	Every value in (4.4) is at most four, so every vertex of every
   275	`D_4,...,D_9` remains available under the sharper cap.  More strongly, at
   276	the numerical Cartier/curvette level all these types have the uniform
   277	transverse one-germ pattern
   278	
   279	```text
   280	n=e_2,
   281	m=(1,2,2,...,2,1,1),                                (4.5)
   282	```
   283	
   284	where the string of twos runs from vertex `2` through vertex `r-2`.
   285	This has `sum n_i=1` and gives a locally tree-shaped attachment.
   286	
   287	## 5. The global intersection caps
   288	
   289	The semigroups in Sections 3--4 are infinite without a global proper-
   290	intersection input: scaling either (3.5) or (4.5) gives an infinite ray.
   291	The class calculation supplies an exact finite cap in the finite stratum.
   292	
   293	Suppose there is a target line through `pi(p)` whose pullback `L~A` and
   294	`R_X` have no common curve.  A projectively finite `pi` guarantees such a
   295	choice: the finitely many images of different components exclude only
   296	finitely many lines through `pi(p)`.  Over every singular point `q in L`,
   297	write
   298	
   299	```text
   300	r^*L=L_str+sum_i ell_(q,i)E_(q,i),       ell_(q,i)>=1. (5.1)
   301	```
   302	
   303	The lower bound holds because the divisorial valuation of every exceptional
   304	curve centred at `q` is positive on the maximal ideal, and the local line
   305	equation belongs to that ideal.
   306	The same orthogonality calculation gives a nonnegative attachment vector
   307	`b_q=C_q ell_q` for `L_str`.  Put `n_q=C_qm_q` for the different.  The three
   308	exceptional cross-terms cancel to one copy:
   309	
   310	```text
   311	L_str.(m_q E_q)       =ell_q^t n_q,
   312	(ell_q E_q).R_str     =ell_q^t n_q,
   313	(ell_q E_q).(m_q E_q)=-ell_q^t n_q.                 (5.2)
   314	```
   315	
   316	Consequently the exact proper-intersection identity is
   317	
   318	```text
   319	8=L.R=L_str.R_str+sum_(q in L intersect Sing X) ell_q^t n_q. (5.3)
   320	```
   321	
   322	The first term and every summand are nonnegative, and every
   323	`ell_(q,i)>=1`.  Therefore, for the chosen point `p`,
   324	
   325	```text
   326	1<=sum_i n_i<=8.                                    (5.4)
   327	```
   328	
   329	There is a sharper conditional slice.  Let `F_p~B` be the `P1`-base fibre
   330	through `p`.  If `F_p` and `R_X` have no common irreducible component, write
   331	`r^*F_p=F_str+sum beta_(q,i)E_(q,i)` at the singular points of that fibre.
   332	The coefficients over `p` are at least one.  The identical cross-term
   333	calculation gives
   334	
   335	```text
   336	4=F_p.R=F_str.R_str+sum_(q in F_p intersect Sing X) beta_q^t n_q,
   337	```
   338	
   339	and properness makes all terms nonnegative.  Hence
   340	
   341	```text
   342	sum_i n_i<=4.                                       (5.5)
   343	```
   344	
   345	Neither cap is asserted when the chosen slice shares a different component.
   346	In particular, projective coefficient basepoints can force every target line
   347	through their image to contain a contracted different carrier.  Those
   348	nonfinite cases remain outside (5.4), although (5.5) can still apply when the
   349	base fibre is proper.
   350	
   351	The fixed infinity divisor gives a coupled boundary/different constraint.
   352	Assume `H` and `R_X` have no common irreducible curve.  At every singular
   353	point `p in H`, use the vectors `(h,a)` from (1.6) and `(m,n)` from (1.2).
   354	Expansion on the resolution gives the exact global identity
   355	
   356	```text
   357	8=H.R=H_str.R_str+sum_(p in H intersect Sing X) h_p^t n_p.  (5.6)
   358	```
   359	
   360	All terms are nonnegative under the no-common-curve hypothesis.  Hence
   361	
   362	```text
   363	sum_p h_p^t n_p<=8,
   364	sum_p sum_i n_(p,i)<=8.                             (5.7)
   365	```
   366	
   367	If `H` and `R_X` share a nonexceptional curve, the proper-intersection
   368	argument behind (5.6)--(5.7) is unavailable.  That common-carrier stratum is
   369	an explicit surviving threat, not silently discarded.
   370	
   371	## 6. Forest topology uses physical germs, not `n`
   372	
   373	Take a full embedded log resolution of the reduced first-leg boundary.  Over
   374	one singular point, include in `T_p` the ADE tree and every additional
   375	exceptional vertex introduced to resolve its boundary contacts.  It is a
   376	connected tree.  Delete the interior of `T_p` from the resolved dual graph.
   377	Then the exact forest criterion is:
   378	
   379	> Every connected component of the remaining graph may meet `T_p` by at
   380	> most one edge.                                                   (6.1)
   381	
   382	Indeed two such edges, together with the unique path inside `T_p` and a path
   383	inside that outside component, form a cycle.  Conversely, attaching forest
   384	components once to a tree preserves a forest.
   385	
   386	The edges in (6.1) are distinct **physical branch contacts after log
   387	resolution**.  They are not the integers `n_i`.
   388	
   389	Consequently the forest kills:
   390	
   391	* two distinct physical branches of one irreducible strict different
   392	  component attaching to the same exceptional tree;
   393	* attachments from two vertices or points whose carriers are already joined
   394	  by a boundary path outside that tree; and
   395	* two disjoint connector loci between the connected total transforms of
   396	  infinity and of the different, unless those supports share a component
   397	  that changes the quotient graph.
   398	
   399	It does not kill:
   400	
   401	* one tangent branch with `n_i>1` at a single physical point;
   402	* one branch through an ADE node, with `n` supported on the two adjacent
   403	  vertices; or
   404	* several branches on distinct outside components which are connected only
   405	  through `T_p`, producing a star rather than a cycle.
   406	
   407	There is a useful global formulation.  In a forest, two connected subgraphs
   408	have connected intersection after subdividing physical intersection points
   409	as vertices.  The support of `H` is connected because it is an effective
   410	ample `(2,3)` divisor on the infinity surface `P1 times P1`; the support of
   411	`R_X` is connected because `2A+B` is ample on `X`.  Their total transforms
   412	are therefore connected.  A survivor with no shared nonexceptional carrier
   413	has only one graph-theoretic connector between them.  The eight units in
   414	`H.R=8` may nevertheless concentrate at that connector as tangency or ADE
   415	Cartier multiplicity.  Numerical weight eight is not eight graph edges.
   416	
   417	## 7. Exact killed/surviving threat table
   418	
   419	The deductions can now be stated without mixing levels.
   420	
   421	```text
   422	KILLED UNCONDITIONALLY BY THE D9 ROOT MODEL
   423	  connected A9, E6, E7, E8;
   424	  any rank-nine direct sum failing det=4*square.
   425	
   426	KILLED IN A CAPPED PROPER-INTERSECTION STRATUM
   427	  every n violating (3.1) or (4.2);
   428	  every n with sum n_i>8 under (5.4);
   429	  every n with sum n_i>4 under (5.5).
   430	
   431	KILLED BY THE RATIONAL-FOREST GRAPH
   432	  precisely the repeated physical joining patterns in (6.1),
   433	  including two contacts from the same outside connected carrier.
   434	
   435	NOT KILLED BY THESE TESTS
   436	  A1,...,A8 via (3.5);
   437	  D4,...,D9 via (4.5);
   438	  high contact concentrated at one physical germ;
   439	  nonfinite/common-carrier cases where no cap has been proved.
   440	```
   441	
   442	The word “survivor” is deliberately negative: it means that the displayed
   443	necessary tests have not contradicted the decoration.  Standard coordinate
   444	roots exhibit an abstract lattice embedding, but do not prove simultaneous
   445	effectivity, realization by vertical curves in the nine-blowup marking, or
   446	compatibility with a quadratic Miranda incidence.
   447	
   448	## 8. Hostile checks, exact remaining gap, and scope firewall
   449	
   450	The following tempting shortcuts are false and have been excluded from the
   451	theorem.
   452	
   453	* **Rank at most nine allows `E6` or `E7`.**  The signed-coordinate proof in
   454	  Section 2 uses the actual roots of `D9`, not rank alone, and removes them.
   455	* **`n_i=4` means four boundary edges.**  It can be one order-four physical
   456	  contact.  Forest topology sees the resolved branch path.
   457	* **Support on two ADE vertices means two physical points.**  A branch
   458	  through their node gives one point, as in the even `A_r` survivor (3.5).
   459	* **Every congruence solution occurs.**  Equations (3.1) and (4.2) are only
   460	  the Cartier-lattice gate.  The effective cone and analytic incidence map
   461	  remain unproved.
   462	* **`A.R=8` always bounds `sum n`.**  The proof needs a pullback line meeting
   463	  the different properly.  It can fail at a nonfinite coefficient basepoint.
   464	* **Connected infinity and different must meet transversely many times.**
   465	  In a forest their entire intersection must instead collapse to one
   466	  connector; all eight intersection units may be concentrated there.
   467	* **A common infinity/different carrier has nonnegative residual
   468	  intersection.**  Proper intersection has failed, so (5.6) cannot be used.
   469	
   470	The exact remaining finite task in the projectively finite,
   471	no-common-carrier stratum is to enumerate the pairs
   472	
   473	```text
   474	(a,n) in (C Z^r intersect (Z_(>=0)^r minus {0}))^2,
   475	sum n_i<=8,       a^t C^(-1)n<=8,                   (8.1)
   476	```
   477	
   478	together with their partitions into physical analytic branches, the single-
   479	connector forest condition, simultaneous embeddings of all singular trees
   480	in `D9`, and effectivity of the strict carrier classes in the ruled
   481	nine-blowup marking.  Formula (8.1) is finite and exact, but this packet does
   482	not assert that every pair is realized.
   483	
   484	Separately unresolved are: a different component contained in infinity; a
   485	vertical/base fibre contained in the different; projective or affine
   486	coefficient-basepoint nonfiniteness; the local analytic restriction imposed
   487	by an actual bidegree-`(2,3)` equation; and simultaneous effective
   488	realization of the allowed root embeddings.  Nonnormal incidence closures
   489	remain governed by their normalization and conductor packet and are not
   490	mixed into this theorem.
   491	
   492	Also outside are a proof that an arbitrary cubic block has a quadratic
   493	presentation, basis minimization, higher coefficient degree, another block
   494	degree, primitivity, a polynomial map, a counterexample, and JC2.  No CAS,
   495	finite-field sampling, AWS, or `jc2-lean` operation enters this packet.
